<Project>
	<Choose>
		<When Condition="'$(UseProjectReferences)' == 'true'">
			<ItemGroup Condition="'@(NorseRef)' != ''">
				<ProjectReference Include="@(NorseRef->'$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)%(Repo)/src/%(Identity)/%(Identity).csproj')" />
				<ProjectReference Include="@(NorseRef->WithMetadataValue('Generator', 'true')->'$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)%(Repo)/gen/%(Identity).Generator/%(Identity).Generator.csproj')" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
			</ItemGroup>
			<!--
				A NorseRef library that bundles more than one Roslyn generator (the default
				{Identity}.Generator above, plus one that doesn't fit the 1:1 naming convention —
				e.g. Infrastructure.Web.Server ships both its gRPC wiring generator and
				Infrastructure.Web.Server.Xml.Generator) can't express the second one via the
				Generator="true" boolean. NorseGeneratorRef is a separate, empty-by-default opt-in
				item list for exactly that: no matching src/ library, analyzer-only, deliberately
				decoupled from NorseRef's Generator="true" so every existing single-generator
				consumer is untouched.
			-->
			<ItemGroup Condition="'@(NorseGeneratorRef)' != ''">
				<ProjectReference Include="@(NorseGeneratorRef->'$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)%(Repo)/gen/%(Identity)/%(Identity).csproj')" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
			</ItemGroup>
			<ItemGroup Condition="'@(NorseDesignRef)' != ''">
				<ProjectReference Include="@(NorseDesignRef->'$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)%(Repo)/src/%(Identity)/%(Identity).csproj')">
					<PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
				</ProjectReference>
			</ItemGroup>
			<!--
				The Law of the Realms (NORSE070-073) attaches to every workspace compilation
				unconditionally — no NorseRef opt-in, no realm opt-out. Two exclusions: the analyzer
				project itself (self-reference), and any Aspire AppHost ($(IsAspireHost) — set by
				Aspire.AppHost.Sdk's own Sdk.props, true for any current or future AppHost project,
				not just Orchestration.AppHost by name). An AppHost is container-orchestration wiring,
				not shipped application code — it declares which resources run and how they're wired,
				never touches wire formats or PII types, and Aspire's own tooling already warns
				(ASPIRE004) when an analyzer-only ProjectReference shows up among its resources, since
				it can't tell the difference between "a service to orchestrate" and "a build-time-only
				reference." Spec:
				Glitnir/docs/Platform/specs/2026-08-03-realm-dependency-law-compiler-enforcement-design.md
			-->
			<ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Architecture.Analyzers' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Primitives.Analyzers' AND '$(IsAspireHost)' != 'true'">
				<ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Svartalfheim/gen/Architecture.Analyzers/Architecture.Analyzers.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
			</ItemGroup>
			<!--
				Primitives.Analyzers (NORSE060-062 — Result<T> egress, the PII retention gate) is
				bundled into Norse.Primitives's own package (analyzers/dotnet/cs/) rather than
				packed standalone, so in NuGet mode it already attaches unconditionally to any
				consumer of Norse.Primitives — no PackageReference-side opt-in exists or is needed.
				This block is dev-mode's equivalent: an ordinary ProjectReference's OutputItemType
				doesn't propagate through a further ProjectReference the way a NuGet package's
				analyzer asset propagates through the whole reference closure, so a consuming
				project that only reaches Primitives transitively (e.g. via a NorseRef'd sibling
				project, never declaring its own <NorseRef Include="Primitives">) silently never
				saw NORSE061/062 locally — the exact gap that let a PII-bearing test fixture ship
				ungated: 2026-08-04, Urdarbrunnr, feature/protected-pii-converter. Unconditional,
				same shape (and same two exclusions, analyzer self-reference and $(IsAspireHost)) as
				the Architecture.Analyzers block above.
			-->
			<ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Primitives.Analyzers' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'Architecture.Analyzers' AND '$(IsAspireHost)' != 'true'">
				<ProjectReference Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Svartalfheim/gen/Primitives.Analyzers/Primitives.Analyzers.csproj" OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
			</ItemGroup>
		</When>
		<When Condition="'$(ManagePackageVersionsCentrally)' == 'true'">
			<ItemGroup>
				<PackageReference Include="@(NorseRef->'Norse.%(Identity)')" />
				<PackageReference Include="@(NorseDesignRef->'Norse.%(Identity)')">
					<PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
				</PackageReference>
			</ItemGroup>
		</When>
		<Otherwise>
			<ItemGroup>
				<PackageReference Include="@(NorseRef->'Norse.%(Identity)')" Version="$(NorseRefVersion)" />
				<PackageReference Include="@(NorseDesignRef->'Norse.%(Identity)')" Version="$(NorseRefVersion)">
					<PrivateAssets>all</PrivateAssets>
				</PackageReference>
			</ItemGroup>
		</Otherwise>
	</Choose>
	<ItemGroup>
		<!--
			The .NET 11 SDK injects this namespace as an implicit global using; it is a banned wire-format
			root (NORSE070), so the injection is removed at the source platform-wide. Must live at targets
			evaluation time — a props-level Remove precedes the SDK's Include and removes nothing. An
			authored `using System.Net.Http.Json;` still convicts; Midgard/Yggdrasil re-add locally where legal.
		-->
		<Using Remove="System.Net.Http.Json" />
	</ItemGroup>
	<!--
		The duplicate-generator strip target (Gap 2 in the design doc below) lives in the
		Ginnungagap-scattered src/tests Directory.Build.targets templates, not here — it must also
		work in a genuinely standalone checkout with no Bifrost ancestor, which this file can never
		be part of by definition. Full design:
		Glitnir/docs/Platform/specs/2026-07-01-norseref-generator-forwarding-design.md

		The Choose block above is not an implementation detail — it is platform doctrine. Every
		NorseRef item resolves to one of two genuinely different mechanisms depending on
		$(UseProjectReferences), and reasoning about a NorseRef consumer (an analyzer, a build
		target, anything downstream) requires knowing which crossing produced it. Doctrine, and
		the postmortem on the one time this platform's own tooling got that wrong:
		Glitnir/docs/the-two-crossings.md
	-->
</Project>
